--TEST--
`require` used for its value hands back what the target file's own file-scope `return` returned, `1` where it returned nothing at all, and a fresh value every time the site is reached (`rule:statements/a-require-expression-is-mixed` and `rule:statements/a-required-file-shares-declarations-not-locals`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:programs/require-run-another-file-in-this-frame
// `rule:statements/a-require-expression-is-mixed`: `require`'s value is what a `return`-ing target hands back,
// typed `mixed` at the boundary because it cannot be known statically — so
// a typed binding takes the same explicit `as` any other `mixed` boundary
// value needs (`rule:types/conversion`'s one unchecked position).
mixed $v = require 'gives.nvs';
echo "value=", $v as string, "\n";

// § 3's other half: a file that never `return`s hands back `1`, PHP's own
// answer for a `require` of such a file. Its statements still run, in place,
// which is what the line above the value proves.
mixed $p = require 'plain.nvs';
echo "plain=", $p as string, "\n";

// The frame is the *file's*, not this one's — declarations cross a `require`
// and variables do not (§ *Decision*) — so the target's `$part` below is not
// this file's, and this one is untouched by having required it.
string $part = "entry";
mixed $again = require 'gives.nvs';
echo "part=", $part, "\n";

// "Executing every time control reaches it" (§ *Decision*), so a site reached
// twice returns twice: the target counts its own runs through a static, which
// is storage a declaration owns rather than a frame.
mixed $one = require 'counts.nvs';
mixed $two = require 'counts.nvs';
echo "runs=", $one as string, ",", $two as string, "\n";

// The same site inside a loop body, for the same reason.
int $i = 0;
for (; $i < 2; $i++) {
    mixed $each = require 'counts.nvs';
    echo "loop=", $each as string, "\n";
}

class Runs {
    public static int $n = 0;
}
--FILE gives.nvs--
<?nvs
string $part = "handed";
echo "gives ran\n";
return $part . " back";
--FILE plain.nvs--
<?nvs
echo "plain ran\n";
--FILE counts.nvs--
<?nvs
Runs::$n = Runs::$n + 1;
return Runs::$n;
--EXPECT--
gives ran
value=handed back
plain ran
plain=1
gives ran
part=entry
runs=1,2
loop=3
loop=4
